    "_comment": "The input-tracking framing shared by engine/ and Website/. The engine writes report_input.data in this framing and the website is the only thing that reads it back; this file is copied verbatim into the website, whose decoder must turn `chunk.data` into exactly `events`. Records: a one-byte type then a big-endian payload - 1 camera (p2 pitch, p2 yaw), 2 applet focus (p1), 3 mouse click (p4, the client word (delta50ms << 20) | (button << 19) | (y * 765 + x)), 4 mouse move (p1 length then the 2/3/4-byte cursor encoding the client writes), 5 time anchor (p2 ticks since the chunk began), 6 marker (p1 reason). Timing: `t` is milliseconds from `chunk.started_at`; a time anchor sets the arrival clock to ticks * 600; camera, focus, click and marker records take the arrival clock in force; a move record has samples that END at it, so sample k of the N samples the record accounts for sits at T - (N - 1 - k) * 50, where an encoded step accounts for delta + 1 samples (delta where the cursor did not move, then the one where it did). A move step is relative until an absolute one has been seen, so x and y are null until then; pos 524287 means the cursor left the applet and decodes as (-1, -1). This chunk is a real one: seventeen one-record chunks were rotated through the sixteen-chunk ring first, which is why it opens with marker 3. Markers 2 and 4 were written directly - the flood cap needs 8 KB of input and the live tail needs a report - so a decoder must handle a marker anywhere in a chunk, not only at its head.",
